\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N$, $\mathbb Z$, $\mathbb Q$, $\mathbb R$, and $\mathbb C$ denote the natural numbers, integers, rationals, reals, and complex numbers, respectively. All additional parameters and hypotheses are specified in the question.


\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For a smooth compact hypersurface with nonzero curvature in $\mathbb R^d$, prove the conjectured sharp $L^p$--$L^q$ Fourier restriction bounds in every dimension $d\geq3$. The restriction operator sends a Schwartz function to the restriction of its Fourier transform to the hypersurface; the requested range is the one dictated by the standard scaling and concentration obstructions.
\par\smallskip\textit{Formulation references:} \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}, \href{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2022/31}{[S2]}.

\subsection{Short English statement}
For a smooth compact hypersurface with nonzero curvature in $\mathbb R^d$, prove the conjectured sharp $L^p$--$L^q$ Fourier restriction bounds in every dimension $d\geq3$. The restriction operator sends a Schwartz function to the restriction of its Fourier transform to the hypersurface; the requested range is the one dictated by the standard scaling and concentration obstructions.

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 30005163 (OWR-11101912-012). Catalogue attribution under CC BY 4.0.
\url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Original problem formulation and mathematical context.
\url{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2022/31}.
\end{itemize}
\label{end:30005163}

